note left of App
  Start-up class: creates the tables and
  enables row-level security, then mounts
  the /api and /api/me routers.
end note

note bottom of ApiClient
  The only HTTP client: every page reaches the
  controllers through it and the FastAPI routers
  /api (public) and /api/me (Google sign-in) (NFR-5).
end note

legend right
  |= Design pattern |= Where |= What it does for us |
  | Boundary-Control-Entity | whole system | pages, controllers and entities kept separate |
  | Factory | Database.make_engine() | picks SQLite or PostgreSQL from DATABASE_URL |
  | Singleton | Settings / get_settings() | one cached configuration object |
  | Facade | ApiClient | one entry point hides HTTP, tokens and errors |
  | Adapter | Presenters | turns entities into API response shapes |
  | Observer | ContextProviders | pages update when sign-in, settings or location change |
  | Template Method | SyncRunLogger.sync_run() | same logging and rollback around every data source |
  | Cache-Aside | DataCache.cached() | builds centre data once per data version, reused until the next sync |
  | Dependency Injection | AuthenticationController | FastAPI Depends() injects the user and db session |
endlegend
